    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This research has a variety of practical applications, as fractures frequently control gas and fluid conductivity and the mechanical behavior of crustal rock. A clearer understanding of the fracture process should enable the geologist to better constrain possible subsurface fracture geometries based on surface and borehole data. It may also suggest new strategies for well placement during the hydraulic fracturing of wells to improve reservoir potential. Finally, based on this research we conclude that one can obtain information on both paleostress direction and magnitude if overlapping fracture geometries are carefully measured in the field.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
